{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778596402899,"sessionID":"ses_1e3656ab8ffekKAyjoWX4SIys7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ab0f8c68ea22e7f7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json"},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\n2:   \"body\": \" related #2097 \\n\\n**CL term**\\nfibrocyte \\n\\n**Updated CL term**\\ncirculating fibrocyte \\n\\nNarrow synonym - monocyte-derived fibrocyte \\n\\n**Suggested revision of textual definition**\\n\\nA hematopoietic-derived cell that predominately develops from myeloid lineage-restricted progenitor cells in bone marrow (Curnow et al., 2010) and circulates in peripheral blood, characterized by co-expression of hematopoietic markers CD45 and CD34 along with stromal markers including collagen type I in both humans and mice (Chesney et al., 1997; Blakaj and Bucala, 2012). This spindle-shaped cell exhibits a unique dual identity, functionally bridging immune and stromal compartments with characteristics of both monocytes and fibroblasts. Predominantly arising from circulating monocytes, it migrates to injury sites via chemokine receptors and participates in wound repair through extracellular matrix deposition, cytokine secretion, and antigen presentation via MHC class II. As mesenchymal progenitors, fibrocytes can differentiate into fibroblasts, myofibroblasts, and adipocytes, contributing to both physiological repair and pathological fibrosis (Blakaj and Bucala, 2012).\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1186/1755-1536-5-S1-S6\\nPMID:9177213\\nPMID:[20303382](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20303382/)\\nPMID: 31473260\\nPMID: [29286323](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29286323/)\\nPMID: [20305780](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20305780/)\\nPMID: 32084275\\n\\n\\nlogical def\\n\\n\\nSubclassOf \\n'stromal cell'\\nprogenitor cell\\n and ('capable of' some 'antigen processing and presentation of peptide antigen via MHC class II')\\n and ('capable of' some 'positive regulation of angiogenesis') and ('capable of' some 'wound healing')\\n'develops from' some 'bone marrow hematopoietic cell' OR \\n'develops from' some 'myeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell' - more specific \\n\\n\\n**REFINE THE COMMENTS SECTION** \\nonly keep information supported by literature - maybe use paperqa2 for this as a huge list... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n3:   \"comments\": [],\n4:   \"title\": \"[Text and logical def] fibrocyte\",\n5:   \"url\": \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3243\"\n6: }\n\n(End of file - total 6 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\n  \"body\": \" related #2097 \\n\\n**CL term**\\nfibrocyte \\n\\n**Updated CL term**\\ncirculating fibrocyte \\n\\nNarrow synonym - monocyte-derived fibrocyte \\n\\n**Suggested revision of textual definition**\\n\\nA hematopoietic-derived cell that predominately develops from myeloid lineage-restricted progenitor cells in bone marrow (Curnow et al., 2010) and circulates in peripheral blood, characterized by co-expression of hematopoietic markers CD45 and CD34 along with stromal markers including collagen type I in both humans and mice (Chesney et al., 1997; Blakaj and Bucala, 2012). This spindle-shaped cell exhibits a unique dual identity, functionally bridging immune and stromal compartments with characteristics of both monocytes and fibroblasts. Predominantly arising from circulating monocytes, it migrates to injury sites via chemokine receptors and participates in wound repair through extracellular matrix deposition, cytokine secretion, and antigen presentation via MHC class II. As mesenchymal progenitors, fibrocytes can differentiate into fibroblasts, myofibroblasts, and adipocytes, contributing to both physiological repair and pathological fibrosis (Blakaj and Bucala, 2012).\\n\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1186/1755-1536-5-S1-S6\\nPMID:9177213\\nPMID:[20303382](h